  <!-- Google Tag Manager -->
  <% if (typeof gtmId !== 'undefined' && gtmId) { %>
  <script nonce="<%= cspNonce %>">(function(w,d,s,l,i){w[l]=w[l]||[];w[l].push({'gtm.start':
  new Date().getTime(),event:'gtm.js'});var f=d.getElementsByTagName(s)[0],
  j=d.createElement(s),dl=l!='dataLayer'?'&l='+l:'';j.async=true;j.src=
  'https://www.googletagmanager.com/gtm.js?id='+i+dl;f.parentNode.insertBefore(j,f);
  })(window,document,'script','dataLayer','<%= gtmId %>');</script>
  <% } %>

  <!-- GA4 -->
  <% const _gaId = (typeof ga4Id !== 'undefined' && ga4Id) ? ga4Id : _seo.google_analytics_id; %>
  <%/* gtag.js is fetched WITH THE PAGE as of 2026-09-30. It previously waited
       for the visitor's first interaction, or 3s, whichever came first.

       WHY THE LOADER EXISTED, AND THE NUMBER IT WAS BUILT ON
       Measured 2026-09-22 on throttled mobile (5 runs each): loading gtag with
       the page cost ~250ms of LCP (2744ms vs 2492ms median) plus the page's
       only long main-thread task (86ms), because 162KB of async script
       competes for bandwidth with the hero image on a slow connection.

       WHY IT WAS REMOVED ANYWAY — Sam's call, 2026-09-30, to re-measure the
       cost against the page as it stands today rather than as it stood on
       2026-09-22. A great deal has shipped since: the four blocking CSS files
       were consolidated into site-bundle.css, the hero srcset ladder was
       capped, the 1x1 data-URI placeholders were dropped, Google Fonts was
       removed entirely, and the forced reflows in site.js were replaced with
       a ResizeObserver. Several of those attacked the same bottleneck the
       loader was working around, so the 250ms may no longer be there to pay.

       DO NOT restore the loader on the strength of the 2026-09-22 number
       alone — that measurement describes a page that no longer exists. Take a
       fresh throttled-mobile reading first. If the cost is still real, the
       loader is in git history at f89af87 and can come back intact.

       WHAT WAS GIVEN UP BY REMOVING IT, deliberately: nothing on the data
       side. The loader's known trade was the opposite direction — a visitor
       who left within ~3s without touching anything was never counted. Eager
       loading counts them. The dataLayer queue below still runs before the
       injection, so the pageview keeps the real URL and referrer either way.

       CSP: the injected <script> carries no nonce and does not need one —
       'strict-dynamic' trusts scripts added by an already-trusted script. */%>
  <% if (_gaId && !(typeof gtmId !== 'undefined' && gtmId)) { %>
  <script nonce="<%= cspNonce %>">
  window.dataLayer=window.dataLayer||[];function gtag(){dataLayer.push(arguments);}
  gtag('js',new Date());gtag('config','<%= _gaId %>');
  (function(){
    var s=document.createElement('script');
    s.async=true;s.src='https://www.googletagmanager.com/gtag/js?id=<%= _gaId %>';
    document.head.appendChild(s);
  })();
  </script>
  <% } %>

  <!-- Microsoft Clarity -->
  <%/* Session recordings and heatmaps, added 2026-09-30 as a second opinion
       alongside GA4 while the in-house KPI dashboards are built.

       EAGER — MICROSOFT'S STOCK SNIPPET, VERBATIM. This was first shipped
       deferred, on the same first-interaction-or-3s loader as GA4 above, to
       protect the measured LCP win (~250ms on throttled mobile, plus the
       page's only long main-thread task). Clarity then never registered the
       install, across a multi-page phone session that included reaching the
       cart — where Sam's prior installs have always registered in minutes.

       The deferral was the one variable we had introduced, so it is removed
       to isolate it. This is now byte-for-byte the snippet Microsoft issues.

       IF IT REGISTERS: the deferral was the cause — Clarity's install
       detection evidently wants the tag at load time — and the LCP question
       becomes a real trade to decide rather than a free win.

       IF IT STILL DOES NOT: the deferral was never the problem, and the
       cause is outside this file. Everything else here is already verified:
       the tag renders with the right id, the script serves correctly from an
       independent network, and CSP permits it. What has NEVER been observed
       is a successful in-browser fetch — Sam's Mac returns 503 from a Chrome
       extension (proven: blocked as a script subresource, allowed as a
       top-level navigation), and iOS Safari blocks known trackers by default
       in Private Browsing. So it is entirely possible no clean browser has
       ever loaded it. Establish that before changing anything else here.

       CSP: the injected <script> needs no nonce — 'strict-dynamic' trusts
       scripts added by an already-trusted script. Note the bootstrap's first
       act is (new Image).src = 'https://c.clarity.ms/c.gif', which is
       governed by img-src ('https:', a blanket allow), NOT the connect-src
       entries added for the beacons. */%>
  <% if (typeof clarityId !== 'undefined' && clarityId) { %>
  <script nonce="<%= cspNonce %>">
    (function(c,l,a,r,i,t,y){
        c[a]=c[a]||function(){(c[a].q=c[a].q||[]).push(arguments)};
        t=l.createElement(r);t.async=1;t.src="https://www.clarity.ms/tag/"+i;
        y=l.getElementsByTagName(r)[0];y.parentNode.insertBefore(t,y);
    })(window, document, "clarity", "script", "<%= clarityId %>");
  </script>
  <% } %>
  <meta name="csrf-token" content="<%= csrfToken %>">
</head>
